The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!folio_test_uptodate(folio)) {
    folio_unlock(folio);
    ret = -EFAULT; /* make the VM retry the fault */
    goto out;
}
```
```c
folio_wait_stable(folio);
```
Reason:
The first statement checks if the folio is up-to-date; if not, it unlocks the folio and returns an error, which can lead to a situation where the folio is not properly synchronized during writeback. The second statement, `folio_wait_stable(folio)`, is conditional on the backing device's stable write requirement, allowing data to be modified while the folio is still in the middle of writeback. This can cause a deadlock when the log writer thread attempts to lock the same folio being written back.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
+	folio_wait_writeback(folio);
```
Reason:
This statement replaces the previous conditional wait with a definitive wait for the writeback to finish, regardless of the stable write requirement of the backing device. This ensures that no modifications can occur while the folio is being written back, thus preventing the deadlock situation that arises when the log writer thread tries to lock the folio during its writeback process.

